### Step 2 — Create `buildah-hl` ClusterBuildStrategy

The built-in `buildah` strategy does **not** mount the corporate CA → x509 errors pulling from `quay.hobbylobby.corp`.

**2a. Export the built-in strategy:**

```bash
oc get clusterbuildstrategy buildah -o yaml > buildah-hl.yaml
```

**2b. Edit `buildah-hl.yaml`:**

| # | Location in file | Edit |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `metadata.name` | `buildah` → `buildah-hl` |
| 2 | `metadata` block | Delete `resourceVersion`, `uid`, `creationTimestamp`, `generation`, and the entire `status:` block if present |
| 3 | `spec.volumes` (bottom of spec; create the list if absent) | Append the CA volume (below) |
| 4 | Each step under `spec.steps` → `volumeMounts` | Append the CA volumeMount (below) |
| 5 | The main build step's script (`args` → the `set -euo pipefail` shell block) | Insert `update-ca-trust extract` as the first line after `set -euo pipefail` |

Volume (edit #3):

```yaml
  volumes:
    - name: trust-manager-ca
      configMap:
        name: hobbylobby.corp
        defaultMode: 420
```

VolumeMount (edit #4):

```yaml
        - mountPath: /etc/pki/ca-trust/extracted/trust-manager
          name: trust-manager-ca
          readOnly: true
```

> These mirror the working pattern in `s2i.yaml` (see its `volumeMounts` and `volumes` sections and the `update-ca-trust extract` line at the top of each step script).

**2c. Apply and verify:**

```bash
oc apply -f buildah-hl.yaml
oc get clusterbuildstrategy buildah-hl
```

---

### Step 3 — Fix the runtime Build in `rfipconfig-be.yaml`

**File:** `rfipconfig-be.yaml` — **second Build** (`rfipconfig-be-runtime-build`).

| # | Field | Current | Change to | Why |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `spec.source.contextDir` | *(empty)* | `runtime` | Point at the new Git directory from Step 1 |
| 2 | `spec.paramValues[0]` (`dockerfile`) | `Dockerfile` | *(keep)* | Path is relative to `contextDir` |
| 3 | `spec.paramValues[1]` (`builder-image`) | `image-registry...rfipconfig-be-builder:<need to be filled>` | **Delete both lines** | `builder-image` is an S2I param; the buildah strategy has no such param — the FROM is hardcoded in the Dockerfile |
| 4 | `spec.strategy.name` | `buildah-hl` | *(keep)* | Now exists after Step 2 |

Resulting runtime Build spec (final form):

```yaml
apiVersion: shipwright.io/v1beta1
kind: Build
metadata:
  name: rfipconfig-be-runtime-build
  namespace: openshift-builds
spec:
  env:
    - name: SSL_CERT_FILE
      value: /etc/pki/ca-trust/extracted/trust-manager/ca-certs.pem
  source:
    type: Git
    git:
      url: git@hlgithub.hobbylobby.corp:Warehouse/rfipconfig.git
      cloneSecret: github-repo-ssh-key-rfipconfig-be
      revision: master
      depth: 1
    contextDir: runtime
  strategy:
    name: buildah-hl
    kind: ClusterBuildStrategy
  paramValues:
    - name: dockerfile
      value: Dockerfile
  output:
    image: image-registry.openshift-image-registry.svc:5000/openshift-builds/rfipconfig-be:latest
    pushSecret: pipeline-push-secret
```

Apply and verify registration:

```bash
oc apply -f rfipconfig-be.yaml
oc get builds.shipwright.io -n openshift-builds
# Both Builds must show REGISTERED=True. (Never plain `oc get build` — that hits build.openshift.io)
```

---

### Step 4 — Create BuildRun files

**File:** `buildrun-runtime.yaml` (new). Use `generateName` so reruns don't collide — and therefore `oc create`, never `oc apply`:

```yaml
apiVersion: shipwright.io/v1beta1
kind: BuildRun
metadata:
  generateName: rfipconfig-be-runtime-build-
  namespace: openshift-builds
spec:
  build:
    name: rfipconfig-be-runtime-build
  serviceAccount: pipeline
```

(Optionally convert the existing `buildrun.yaml` for the S2I build to `generateName: rfipconfig-be-s2i-build-` the same way.)

---

### Step 5 — Run the sequence

```bash
# 1. Builder image (S2I)
oc create -f buildrun.yaml -n openshift-builds

# 2. Watch until Succeeded=True
oc get buildruns.shipwright.io -n openshift-builds -w

# 3. Runtime image (buildah) — only after step 2 succeeds
oc create -f buildrun-runtime.yaml -n openshift-builds
oc get buildruns.shipwright.io -n openshift-builds -w
```

> ⚠️ **OCPBUGS-84214** (operator v1.8.1): do not open the BuildRun detail page in the web console — it crashes with React error #310. Use CLI only:

```bash
oc describe buildrun <name> -n openshift-builds
oc logs -f -n openshift-builds -l buildrun.shipwright.io/name=<name> --all-containers
# or: shp buildrun logs <name> -n openshift-builds --follow
```

**Verify the output image:**

```bash
oc get istag rfipconfig-be:latest -n openshift-builds
```

